{"pattern_name": "Uninitialized Data Structure", "functionality": "The code processes a query string by tokenizing it and extracting key-value pairs, which are then decoded and stored in a Lua table.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code does not check if the value after the '=' character in the token is non-empty before processing it, which can lead to undefined behavior or incorrect processing.", "vulnerable_example": "if(strncmp(tok, \"csrf\", strlen(\"csrf\")) && (_equal = strchr(tok, '='))) {", "fixing_pattern": "The fix adds a check to ensure that the value after the '=' character is non-empty before proceeding with further processing.", "fixing_example": "if(strncmp(tok, \"csrf\", strlen(\"csrf\")) && (_equal = strchr(tok, '=')) && (strlen(_equal) > 1)) {"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure File Path Validation", "functionality": "The code attempts to open and validate a directory by checking its existence and permissions without properly validating the input path.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code uses the `stat` function to check the existence and permissions of a directory but does not validate the input path to ensure it is safe and does not contain malicious components.", "vulnerable_example": "struct dump_dir *dd_opendir(const char *dir, int flags)\n{\n    struct dump_dir *dd = dd_init();\n    dir = dd->dd_dirname = rm_trailing_slashes(dir);\n    struct stat stat_buf;\n    if (stat(dir, &stat_buf) != 0)\n        goto cant_access;\n    dd->mode = (stat_buf.st_mode & 0666);\n    errno = 0;\n    if (dd_lock(dd, WAIT_FOR_OTHER_PROCESS_USLEEP, flags) < 0)\n    {\n        if ((flags & DD_OPEN_READONLY) && errno == EACCES)\n        {\n            if (stat(dir, &stat_buf) == 0\n             && S_ISDIR(stat_buf.st_mode)\n             && access(dir, R_OK) == 0\n            ) {\n                if(dd_check(dd) != NULL)\n                {\n                    dd_close(dd);\n                    dd = NULL;\n                }\n                return dd;\n            }\n        }\n        if (errno == EISDIR)\n        {\n            error_msg(\"'%s' is not a problem directory\", dir);\n        }\n        else\n        {\n cant_access:\n            if (errno == ENOENT || errno == ENOTDIR)\n            {\n                if (!(flags & DD_FAIL_QUIETLY_ENOENT))\n                    error_msg(\"'%s' does not exist\", dir);\n            }\n            else\n            {\n                if (!(flags & DD_FAIL_QUIETLY_EACCES))\n                    perror_msg(\"Can't access '%s'\", dir);\n            }\n        }\n        dd_close(dd);\n        return NULL;\n    }\n    dd->dd_uid = (uid_t)-1L;\n    dd->dd_gid = (gid_t)-1L;\n    if (geteuid() == 0)\n    {\n        struct stat stat_buf;\n        if (stat(dir, &stat_buf) != 0 || !S_ISDIR(stat_buf.st_mode))\n        {\n            error_msg(\"Can't stat '%s', or it is not a directory\", dir);\n            dd_close(dd);\n            return NULL;\n        }\n        dd->dd_uid = stat_buf.st_uid;\n        dd->dd_gid = stat_buf.st_gid;\n    }\n    return dd;\n}", "fixing_pattern": "The fix introduces a function `dd_exist` that validates the file name using `str_is_correct_filename` before checking its existence. This ensures that only safe and valid file names are processed.", "fixing_example": "int dd_exist(const struct dump_dir *dd, const char *name)\n{\n    if (!str_is_correct_filename(name))\n        error_msg_and_die(\"Cannot test existence. '%s' is not a valid file name\", name);\n    const int ret = exist_file_dir_at(dd->dd_fd, name);\n    return ret;\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Insufficient Path Validation", "functionality": "The code checks if a given path is valid for mounting a filesystem, but the validation is insufficient to prevent directory traversal.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code fails to properly sanitize or validate user-supplied paths, allowing directory traversal sequences (e.g., '../') to bypass intended restrictions.", "vulnerable_example": "bool mpOk(const std::string& path) {\n    return path.find(\"/home\") == 0;\n}", "fixing_pattern": "Implement proper path validation by resolving the full path and ensuring it remains within the allowed directory tree.", "fixing_example": "bool mpOk(const std::string& path) {\n    std::string resolvedPath = resolvePath(path);\n    return resolvedPath.find(\"/home\") == 0 && !containsTraversal(resolvedPath);\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Improper Path Handling in Shell Commands", "functionality": "The code uses file paths directly in shell commands without proper handling of special characters.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Directly incorporating file paths into shell commands without proper escaping or validation.", "vulnerable_example": "os.system(f'notify-send \"{file_path}\"')", "fixing_pattern": "Using proper escaping mechanisms or safer alternatives that handle special characters correctly.", "fixing_example": "subprocess.run(['notify-send', shlex.quote(file_path)], check=True)"}
{"pattern_name": "Unvalidated Command Arguments", "functionality": "The code collects command-line arguments and uses them to execute a subcommand.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code does not validate the arguments passed to the subcommand, allowing an attacker to inject malicious arguments that could alter the behavior of the executed command.", "vulnerable_example": "let args: Vec<&str> = std::env::args().collect();\nlet subcommand_args = &args[2..];\nlet output = Command::new(subcommand).args(subcommand_args).output()?;", "fixing_pattern": "Validate and sanitize all arguments passed to the subcommand. Use a whitelist of allowed arguments or escape special characters to prevent injection.", "fixing_example": "let allowed_args = [\"arg1\", \"arg2\"];\nlet sanitized_args: Vec<&str> = subcommand_args.iter().filter(|&arg| allowed_args.contains(arg)).collect();\nlet output = Command::new(subcommand).args(sanitized_args).output()?;"}
{"pattern_name": "Unsafe Path Handling in Command Construction", "functionality": "The code constructs a command string that includes a directory path, which is used to change the working directory in a shell command.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code directly appends the directory path to the command string without proper sanitization or escaping, which can lead to command injection or unintended file access.", "vulnerable_example": "sb.append( unifyQuotes( dir ) );", "fixing_pattern": "The fix involves using a method that properly quotes and escapes the directory path to prevent injection or unintended behavior.", "fixing_example": "sb.append( quoteOneItem( dir, false ) );"}
{"pattern_name": "Unsanitized Command Injection", "functionality": "The code constructs a command string using user-controlled input and executes it using the `system` function.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using the `system` function with unsanitized user input, allowing command injection.", "vulnerable_example": "retval = system(cmdline) >> 8;", "fixing_pattern": "Replace the `system` function with a safer alternative that does not allow command injection, such as using `execve` with explicit arguments, or sanitize the input to prevent injection.", "fixing_example": "// Example of using execve with explicit arguments\nchar *args[] = {\"safe_command\", \"arg1\", \"arg2\", NULL};\nexecve(\"/path/to/safe_command\", args, NULL);"}
{"pattern_name": "Missing Authorization in Command Execution", "functionality": "The code executes a shell command, logs the command and its output to files, and optionally modifies the output before returning it.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code lacks proper authorization checks before executing the shell command. While it validates the command and log directory inputs, it does not verify if the user has the necessary permissions to execute the command, leading to potential unauthorized command execution.", "vulnerable_example": "<?php\nfunction executeCommand($command) {\n    // No authorization check before executing the command\n    $output = shell_exec(escapeshellcmd($command));\n    return $output;\n}\n?>", "fixing_pattern": "To resolve this vulnerability, implement an authorization mechanism to ensure that only authorized users can execute the command. This can be done by checking the user's permissions or roles before proceeding with the command execution.", "fixing_example": "<?php\nfunction executeCommand($command) {\n    // Check if the user is authorized to execute the command\n    if (!isAuthorizedUser()) {\n        throw new Exception('Unauthorized command execution.');\n    }\n    $output = shell_exec(escapeshellcmd($command));\n    return $output;\n}\n\nfunction isAuthorizedUser() {\n    // Implement logic to check user authorization\n    return true; // Placeholder for actual authorization logic\n}\n?>"}
{"pattern_name": "Missing End-of-File Check During Array Processing", "functionality": "The code processes elements of an array read from an input stream without checking for end-of-file conditions.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code does not verify if the input stream has reached the end of file (`avio_feof`) while reading array elements, which can lead to reading beyond the valid data.", "vulnerable_example": "for (i = 0; i < segment->nb_index_entries; i++) {\n    segment->temporal_offset_entries[i] = avio_r8(pb);\n    avio_r8(pb);\n    segment->flag_entries[i] = avio_r8(pb);\n    segment->stream_offset_entries[i] = avio_rb64(pb);\n    avio_skip(pb, length - 11);\n}", "fixing_pattern": "The fix adds an end-of-file check before processing each array element to ensure the input stream has sufficient data.", "fixing_example": "for (i = 0; i < segment->nb_index_entries; i++) {\n    if(avio_feof(pb))\n        return AVERROR_INVALIDDATA;\n    segment->temporal_offset_entries[i] = avio_r8(pb);\n    avio_r8(pb);\n    segment->flag_entries[i] = avio_r8(pb);\n    segment->stream_offset_entries[i] = avio_rb64(pb);\n    avio_skip(pb, length - 11);\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Unbounded Loop with Missing EOF Check", "functionality": "The code reads data from a file or stream in a loop without checking for the end of the file (EOF) condition, which can lead to an infinite loop if the expected data is not present.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The loop iterates over data without verifying if the end of the file has been reached, potentially causing an infinite loop if the data is incomplete or corrupted.", "vulnerable_example": "for (j = 0; j < len; j++)\n    av_log(s, AV_LOG_DEBUG, \"%X\", avio_r8(pb));", "fixing_pattern": "Add an EOF check within the loop to ensure that the loop terminates if the end of the file is reached prematurely.", "fixing_example": "for (j = 0; j < len; j++) {\n    if (avio_feof(pb))\n        return AVERROR_INVALIDDATA;\n    av_log(s, AV_LOG_DEBUG, \"%X\", avio_r8(pb));\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Inconsistent Error Handling", "functionality": "The function handles errors inconsistently, sometimes returning directly and sometimes using a common error handling path.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The function returns directly with `-EOPNOTSUPP` in some error cases, bypassing the common error handling path.", "vulnerable_example": "if (!nfp_abm_u32_check_knode(alink->abm, knode, proto, extack))\n  goto err_delete;\n...\nif ((iter->val & cmask) == (val & cmask) &&\n    iter->band != knode->res->classid) {\n  NL_SET_ERR_MSG_MOD(extack, \"conflict with already offloaded filter\");\n  goto err_delete;\n}", "fixing_pattern": "The fix ensures that all error paths consistently use the `err_delete` label and return the appropriate error code.", "fixing_example": "if (!nfp_abm_u32_check_knode(alink->abm, knode, proto, extack)) {\n  err = -EOPNOTSUPP;\n  goto err_delete;\n}\n...\nif ((iter->val & cmask) == (val & cmask) &&\n    iter->band != knode->res->classid) {\n  NL_SET_ERR_MSG_MOD(extack, \"conflict with already offloaded filter\");\n  err = -EOPNOTSUPP;\n  goto err_delete;\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Missing Error Handling Return", "functionality": "The function handles errors by printing an error message but does not return from the function, allowing execution to continue and potentially leading to undefined behavior.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The function does not return after encountering an error, which can lead to further issues such as double freeing memory or accessing invalid resources.", "vulnerable_example": "if ((wbmp = createwbmp (gdImageSX (image), gdImageSY (image), WBMP_WHITE)) == NULL) {\n    gd_error(\"Could not create WBMP\");\n}", "fixing_pattern": "The fix adds a return statement after the error message to ensure the function exits immediately upon encountering an error, preventing further execution of potentially harmful code.", "fixing_example": "if ((wbmp = createwbmp (gdImageSX (image), gdImageSY (image), WBMP_WHITE)) == NULL) {\n    gd_error(\"Could not create WBMP\");\n    return 1;\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Lack of Command Execution Result Validation", "functionality": "The code executes a command but does not properly validate the result, which can lead to ignoring errors or unexpected behavior.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Failing to check the return code or output of a command execution, which can result in ignoring errors or unexpected behavior.", "vulnerable_example": "p = self._buffered_exec_command('dd if=%s bs=%s' % (in_path, BUFSIZE), None)", "fixing_pattern": "Validate the return code and output of command executions to ensure that the command completed successfully and handle errors appropriately.", "fixing_example": "stdout, stderr = p.communicate()\nif p.returncode != 0:\n    raise errors.AnsibleError(\"failed to transfer file to %s:\\n%s\\n%s\" % (out_path, stdout, stderr))"}
{"pattern_name": "Unsafe Use of Function Return Values", "functionality": "The code uses the return values of functions directly without checking if the function execution was successful.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Using function return values directly in subsequent operations without validating the success of the function call.", "vulnerable_example": "return ntlm_av_pair_add(pAvPairList, cbAvPairList, ntlm_av_pair_get_id(pAvPair),\n\tntlm_av_pair_get_value_pointer(pAvPair), ntlm_av_pair_get_len(pAvPair));", "fixing_pattern": "Validating the success of function calls before using their return values in subsequent operations.", "fixing_example": "if (!ntlm_av_pair_get_id(pAvPair, cbAvPair, &pair))\n\treturn FALSE;\nif (!ntlm_av_pair_get_len(pAvPair, cbAvPair, &avLen))\n\treturn FALSE;\nreturn ntlm_av_pair_add(pAvPairList, cbAvPairList, pair,\n\tntlm_av_pair_get_value_pointer(pAvPair), avLen);"}
