{"pattern_name": "Unnecessary Resource Cleanup", "functionality": "The code performs cleanup operations for resources that may not need to be explicitly managed, leading to potential inefficiencies or errors.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code includes a redundant call to `free(big_buffer)` without checking if the buffer is still in use or if it has already been freed. This can lead to double-free vulnerabilities or memory corruption.", "vulnerable_example": "(void) free(big_buffer);", "fixing_pattern": "The fix removes the unnecessary call to `free(big_buffer)` to avoid potential double-free issues or memory corruption.", "fixing_example": "// Removed redundant free(big_buffer)"}
{"pattern_name": "Resource Leakage Due to Missing Resource Cleanup", "functionality": "The code is responsible for managing resources (e.g., files, streams) but fails to properly close them after use.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code does not ensure that resources are closed after use, which can lead to resource leaks and potential exposure of sensitive data.", "vulnerable_example": "private boolean isStale(URL source, File target) {\n    if( source.getProtocol().equals(\"jar\") ) {\n        try {\n            String parts[] = source.getFile().split(Pattern.quote(\"!\"));\n            source = new URL(parts[0]);\n        } catch (MalformedURLException e) {\n            return false;\n        }\n    }\n    File sourceFile=null;\n    if( source.getProtocol().equals(\"file\") ) {\n        sourceFile = new File(source.getFile());\n    }\n    if( sourceFile!=null && sourceFile.exists() ) {\n        if( sourceFile.lastModified() > target.lastModified() ) {\n            return true;\n        }\n    }\n    return false;\n}", "fixing_pattern": "The fix introduces a method that ensures resources are properly closed after use, preventing resource leaks.", "fixing_example": "static private void close(Closeable file) {\n    if(file!=null) {\n        try {\n            file.close();\n        } catch (Exception ignore) {\n        }\n    }\n}"}
{"pattern_name": "Resource Cleanup Order", "functionality": "The function allocates and releases resources such as GSS buffers and server handles during its execution. Proper cleanup of these resources is critical to avoid memory leaks.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The `gss_release_buffer` calls are placed after the `exit_func` label, which is also used for other cleanup operations. This can lead to resource cleanup being skipped if an early `goto exit_func` occurs, causing memory leaks.", "vulnerable_example": "goto exit_func; ... exit_func: free_server_handle(handle);", "fixing_pattern": "Move the `gss_release_buffer` calls before the `exit_func` label to ensure they are executed regardless of the code path taken. This guarantees proper cleanup of resources.", "fixing_example": "gss_release_buffer(&minor_stat, &client_name); gss_release_buffer(&minor_stat, &service_name); goto exit_func; exit_func: free_server_handle(handle);"}
{"pattern_name": "Unvalidated String Return in Legacy Definition Handling", "functionality": "The code processes a legacy definition string by applying functions and variables, then returns the result as a string value.", "vulnerable_pattern": "Returning potentially unsafe or unvalidated string values from legacy definitions without proper sanitization or validation.", "vulnerable_example": "final String tmp = context.applyFunctionsAndVariables(copy, location, legacyDefinition);\nif (tmp == null) {\n  return TValue.fromString(\"\");\n}\nreturn TValue.fromString(tmp);", "fixing_pattern": "Simplifying the null check and ensuring consistent handling of empty strings to prevent potential SSRF vulnerabilities.", "fixing_example": "final String tmp = context.applyFunctionsAndVariables(copy, location, legacyDefinition);\nif (tmp == null)\n  return TValue.fromString(\"\");\nreturn TValue.fromString(tmp);"}
{"pattern_name": "Insecure File Path Validation", "functionality": "The code attempts to validate access to a problem directory based on a user-provided path without ensuring the path is safe or properly resolved.", "vulnerable_pattern": "The code directly uses the user-provided `problem_id` path to check accessibility and open the directory, which can lead to path traversal or symlink attacks.", "vulnerable_example": "if (!dump_dir_accessible_by_uid(problem_id, caller_uid))", "fixing_pattern": "The fix introduces a secure approach by first opening the directory using `dd_openfd` to resolve the path safely and then checking accessibility using the file descriptor.", "fixing_example": "int dir_fd = dd_openfd(problem_id);\nif (!fdump_dir_accessible_by_uid(dir_fd, caller_uid))"}
